==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*keh₂p-}}
{{glossary|verlan|Verlan}} (or {{glossary|metathesis}}) form of {{uder|en|fr|chassé}}, past participle of {{m|fr|chasser||chase}}, from {{uder|en|la|captō}}, {{glossary|frequentative}} of {{m|la|capiō||I take}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsæʃeɪ/|/sæˈʃeɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-sashay.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{homophones|en|sachet}}
* {{rhymes|en|æʃeɪ|eɪ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[chassé]].
# A [[sequence]] of sideways steps in a [[circle]] in [[square dancing]].

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[walk]] [[casually]], [[showily]] or in a [[flirty]] manner; to [[strut]], [[swagger]] or [[flounce]].
#* {{RQ:Henry Four Million|chapter=The Brief Début Of Tildy|page=256|passage=“Fresh guy,” explained Aileen, “last night as I was going home at Twenty-third and Sixth. '''Sashayed''' up, so he did, and made a break. I turned him down, cold, and he made a sneak; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:London Star Rover|passage=Some time, when they shift some decent guards on us that will give us a peep at a newspaper, you get yourself thrown into the jacket, climb out of your body, and '''sashay''' down to little old ’Frisco.}}
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Jill the Reckless|passage=“… He got on Forty-second Street, and he was kinda fresh from the start. At Sixty-sixth he came '''sasshaying'''{{sic}} right down the car and said ‘Hello, patootie!’ Well, I drew myself up….”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|author=w:Truman Capote|title=Observations. Photographs by Richard Avedon. Comments by Truman Capote.|page=18|passage=It does amuse to remember the fadeout road of Chaplin's silent days, the empty dusty vista down which, at the end of every adventure, the little bum recedingly '''sashayed''', knapsack aboard.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=w:Walter Dean Myers|title=The Glory Field|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_ePdzF_m3V4C&pg=PA80&dq=%22sashayed%22|isbn=0-590-45898-1|page=80|passage=Goldie pulled her skirts up in front of her, gave Elijah a look over her shoulder, and '''sashayed''' away.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2022/oct/15/love-island-winner-ekin-su-on-sex-spin-offs-and-surgery-interview|title=Love Island winner Ekin-Su on sex, spin-offs and surgery{{...|: ‘I’ve not had anything major done. OK, apart from the boobs’}}|date=2022-10-15|author=Zoe Williams|work=The Guardian|passage=She '''sashayed''' into the villa as just another bombshell, but stole the show.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[chassé]] when [[dancing]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|title=Tom Swift and the Electronic Hydrolung|author=Victor Appleton|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/19258/19258-h/19258-h.htm|passage="Hope I didn't put away too much fried chicken to '''sashay''' properly at the square dance," Bud remarked.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move [[sideways]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 March 2015|author=Louise Taylor|journal=The Guardian|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/mar/04/newcastle-united-manchester-united-premier-league-match-report
|passage=Games can hinge on the sort of controversial decision made by Taylor in the 10th minute. After Rivière collected Gabriel Obertan’s pass and '''sashayed''' beyond Daley Blind he drew the United centre- half into a rash, clumsy challenge but, puzzlingly, Taylor detected no penalty.}}

